      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To explore participants' experiences of psychosocial adjustment within 18-months following amputation in a manner that can inform further research and clinical practice."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Transcript data from eight semi-structured interviews were analysed using Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Three super-ordinate themes emerged from the analysis: (1) pre-amputation decision-making process and control, (2) renegotiation of self-identity and the struggle to accept a new 'disabled' identity, and (3) adjustment as a social process."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "This research furthers understanding of pre-acute, acute and post-acute adjustment processes from the insider-perspective of individuals who have experienced amputation. A picture emerged in which individuals adjusted to amputation and its sequelae through a process of renegotiation of self-identity that was mediated through a wide range of decisional, informational and social factors. Suggestions for clinical practice and further research are discussed."
        }
      ]},
